cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this report or its data, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  Shackled to the Machine: A DeepSeek-R1-8B Record on
  Four Raspberry Pi 5
abstract: >-
  We evaluate CPU-only distributed inference of DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B
  (dense 8B, Q40) on a four-node Raspberry Pi 5 (16 GB) cluster running
  distributed-llama. A clean-room measurement at the optimal thread count
  sustains 8.32 tok/s decode (n=20, 95% CI [8.30, 8.34]), bit-exact -- to our
  knowledge the best published figure for this model on Raspberry Pi 5, 29%
  above the prior four-node record (6.43 tok/s) and 37% above the Seeed Studio
  configuration (6.06 tok/s). We attribute the gain by layer: the production
  binary is byte-identical to clean upstream, so the source/kernel changes
  contribute approximately zero to dense decode (they add 86% to prefill and
  15.2% to a 30B Mixture-of-Experts model in the companion report). Dense decode
  is bound by memory bandwidth; the gains live in the hardware and
  operating-system layers, not the application kernels. We frame inference
  optimisation as a three-layer stack (hardware/firmware, operating system,
  application software) and argue for profiling the binding constraint and
  tuning the layer it lives in. We additionally describe a bit-exact, adaptive
  prompt-lookup speculative decoder and a controlled dense-vs-MoE contrast on
  identical silicon.
